<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaykhusraw II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ghiyath al-Din Kaykhusraw II, Kay Khusraw II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaykhusraw II was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> from 1237 to 1246, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a>. His reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babai-revolt">Babai revolt</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> in 1239–1240 and, in 1243, a crushing defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a>, after which the sultanate became a Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and lost its independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii.jpg" alt="Kaykhusraw II" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaykhusraw_II_dirham.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ghiyath al-Din Kaykhusraw ibn Kayqubad was the son of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> and Mahpari Khatun, a Byzantine Greek by origin. Although he was the eldest, his father chose as heir a younger son, 'Izz al-Din. In 1226 Kayqubad gave him the newly annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, and he took part with the general Kamyar in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a>. He married Tamar, a daughter of Queen Rusudan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, who as Gurju Khatun became Queen of Queens. On Kayqubad's death in 1237 he seized the throne with the support of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and Sa'd al-Din Köpek, who excelled at political murder, guided his early reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kaykhusraw captured Diyarbekir from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> in 1241. At home the preacher Baba Ishak stirred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to revolt from the borderland of Kafarsud in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>. The rebels destroyed Seljuq armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> and took the regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a> before they were defeated near Kırşehir, with Frankish mercenaries credited by Simon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a>. Between 1240 and 1243 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> struck at Sivas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in his name bore a lion and sun, which some read as the constellation Leo, Tamar's sign, and others as the sultan and his wife.</p><p>In 1243 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Bayju took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>. Kaykhusraw gathered allied contingents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and on 26 June 1243 met the Mongols at the pass of Köse Dağ with an army of 80,000. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> disorganised it, and it was routed. The Mongols took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> but did not move on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. His vizier Muhadhdhab al-Din negotiated, and the sultan returned as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> paying a heavy annual tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kaykhusraw died in 1246 leaving three young sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykaus-ii">Kaykaus II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-iv">Kilij Arslan IV</a> and Kayqubad II. He had named Kayqubad his successor, but the vizier Shams al-Din Isfahani set all three on the throne as co-rulers. One account holds that his failure to name a competent successor was the real blow to the dynasty, since power passed from Seljuq princes to court administrators.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaykhusraw II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army’s collapse came at the Battle of Köse Dağ in 1243, when the Mongols under Baiju routed <b>Kaykhusraw II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Mongol army under Baiju defeated Sultan <b>Kaykhusraw II</b> in north-eastern Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Antalya, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.91,30.7&amp;year=1236" aria-label="Antalya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Kaykhusraw II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5048 4411h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5048 4411h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.91,30.7&amp;year=1236">What was here in 1236? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
